WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.475
The Maryland Health Benefit Exchange is the public corporation

00:00:03.475 --> 00:00:06.563
created in 2011 that operates Maryland Health Connection.

00:00:06.563 --> 00:00:08.108
It has two divisions.

00:00:08.108 --> 00:00:13.513
The Individual Exchange sells qualified health plans to individuals and families and is the

00:00:13.513 --> 00:00:18.918
only place to receive premium tax credits and cost-sharing reductions; it also screens applicants

00:00:18.918 --> 00:00:23.551
for Medicaid and the Maryland Children’s Health Program through a single application.

00:00:23.551 --> 00:00:27.025
The SHOP Exchange, Maryland Health Connection for Small Business,

00:00:27.025 --> 00:00:30.114
serves small employers with up to 50 employees.

00:00:30.114 --> 00:00:33.203
Carriers may sell the same plans off the

00:00:33.203 --> 00:00:35.905
Exchange, but subsidies flow only through it.

00:00:35.905 --> 00:00:39.380
Rosa shops the Individual Exchange; Priya may use SHOP.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:45.349
Individual coverage can be bought only during open enrollment or a special enrollment

00:00:45.349 --> 00:00:51.319
period, a rule that fights adverse selection now that coverage is guaranteed issue.

00:00:51.319 --> 00:00:56.829
Maryland sets a longer window than the federal marketplace: open enrollment runs

00:00:56.829 --> 00:01:02.340
from November 1 through January 15 each year, with coverage starting January

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:07.850
1 for enrollments by December 31 and February 1 for later ones.

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:11.064
Medicaid and the children’s program enroll year-round.

00:01:11.064 --> 00:01:15.656
Maryland also runs Easy Enrollment: a checkbox on the state

00:01:15.656 --> 00:01:20.248
tax return lets the Comptroller share information with the Exchange,

00:01:20.248 --> 00:01:24.840
which then opens a special enrollment window for uninsured filers.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:31.174
Outside open enrollment, a qualifying life event opens a special enrollment period, generally

00:01:31.174 --> 00:01:37.508
60 days: losing other coverage, including a job-based plan, COBRA or Maryland continuation

00:01:37.508 --> 00:01:43.842
running out, or turning 26; marriage, birth, adoption or placement; a permanent move

00:01:43.842 --> 00:01:50.176
into Maryland or to a new rating area; release from incarceration; gaining citizenship

00:01:50.176 --> 00:01:56.023
or lawful presence; or certain changes in income that affect subsidy eligibility.

00:01:56.023 --> 00:02:01.383
Theo’s client who leaves a job in May has 60 days

00:02:01.383 --> 00:02:06.742
to enroll with coverage starting the first of the following month.

00:02:06.742 --> 00:02:10.640
Someone who misses both windows waits for November.

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:15.091
In SHOP, Priya decides how much choice her employees get.

00:02:15.091 --> 00:02:17.762
She may offer a single plan.

00:02:17.762 --> 00:02:22.659
She may pick a metal level and let employees choose any

00:02:22.659 --> 00:02:27.555
plan at that level from any participating carrier, the employee choice

00:02:27.555 --> 00:02:31.562
option, setting a reference plan to define her contribution.

00:02:31.562 --> 00:02:35.568
Or she may offer all plans from one carrier.

00:02:35.568 --> 00:02:39.574
Whatever she chooses, she sets a contribution percentage, employees

00:02:39.574 --> 00:02:43.580
enroll online, and SHOP sends her one monthly bill.

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:46.696
SHOP also lets her offer stand-alone dental.

00:02:46.696 --> 00:02:52.483
Employers with fewer than 25 full-time equivalents and average wages under the federal

00:02:52.483 --> 00:02:58.270
threshold may claim the small business health care tax credit for two years.

00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:01.212
The Exchange must screen every applicant for every program.

00:03:01.212 --> 00:03:05.460
An applicant who turns out to be eligible for Medicaid or the children’s

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:09.709
program is referred and enrolled there; a Medicaid applicant whose income is too

00:03:09.709 --> 00:03:13.958
high is referred to the Exchange for a qualified health plan with subsidies.

00:03:13.958 --> 00:03:17.553
Carriers, the Exchange and the Department of Health must coordinate so

00:03:17.553 --> 00:03:21.149
families with mixed eligibility, parents on a marketplace plan and children

00:03:21.149 --> 00:03:24.090
on the children’s program, are handled in one process.

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:27.358
A producer who learns that a client qualifies for Medicaid

00:03:27.358 --> 00:03:30.300
must say so, even though Medicaid pays no commission.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:33.338
Three kinds of people assist Exchange applicants.

00:03:33.338 --> 00:03:36.810
Authorized insurance producers hold a Maryland license, complete

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:39.848
Exchange training and may earn carrier commissions.

00:03:39.848 --> 00:03:44.621
Navigators and certified application counselors work for grant-funded organizations, complete certification

00:03:44.621 --> 00:03:49.395
and may not accept commissions or steer applicants to particular carriers.

00:03:49.395 --> 00:03:53.301
Carriers’ own representatives may enroll people in their plans.

00:03:53.301 --> 00:03:58.509
All of them must give accurate information about every option, protect applicant

00:03:58.509 --> 00:04:03.282
data and never charge the consumer a fee for enrollment help.

00:04:03.282 --> 00:04:07.188
Theo, as an authorized producer, can enroll Rosa in

00:04:07.188 --> 00:04:10.660
a subsidized plan and Priya’s bakery in SHOP.
